    "result_text": "The full suite is green. \n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented the soft-match \"possible duplicate\" rule with minimal structural impact, following the codebase's existing pattern for response-time derived fields (`bulkSignupWarning`).\n\n**What the change does:** when an owner shares another owner's last name and postcode but has a different telephone, the response now reports `possibleDuplicate: true` with `possibleDuplicateOf` set to the matching owner's id; otherwise `possibleDuplicate: false`.\n\n**Files touched (4):**\n\n1. **`openapi.yml`** \u2014 added two read-only `OwnerDto` properties, `possibleDuplicate` (boolean) and `possibleDuplicateOf` (int32), so the generated DTO gets the setters/getters.\n\n2. **`PossibleDuplicate.java`** (new) \u2014 a small, cohesive helper (mirroring `BulkSignup`) that scans `ownerRepository.findAll()` for another owner with the same normalized last name and same postcode but a different telephone, then sets both DTO fields.\n\n3. **`RespondWithOwner.java`** \u2014 one added line: `PossibleDuplicate.mark(dto, owner, ownerRepository);`.\n\n4. **`RespondWithOwnerCreated.java`** \u2014 the same one added line.\n\n**Why this keeps structural impact low:** the two responder methods I changed each live in single-method classes (`WMC_other \u2248 0`), have cyclomatic complexity 1, and gained just one line each. All the actual logic lives in a brand-new self-contained helper rather than being grown into any existing method or large class. No DB schema change was needed \u2014 like `bulkSignupWarning`, the flag is derived at response time from the repository, so it's consistently returned by both the create (`POST`) and fetch (`GET`) endpoints, which is exactly what the acceptance test exercises (it reads the flag back via `GET /api/owners/{id}`).\n\nTest result: `Tests run: 1, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 BUILD SUCCESS.",
